
  Switching the recipe pages to React would also affect:
  - [1mG1, your goal for the site.[0m You said [3m"I want a recipe site that loads instantly on a phone, even on a bad
[0m    [3mconnection."[0m A client-side React bundle adds JavaScript that has to download before the page shows, which
    works against that. React that builds the pages to static HTML ahead of time (using a static site
    generator) avoids most of the cost.
  - [1mT1, the recipe page template.[0m This is what we're working on now, so its approach would change.
  - [1mD2, one Markdown file per recipe.[0m This still works with React.

  How do you want to handle it?
  1. [1mChange it for good:[0m React replaces D1 everywhere.
  2. [1mOne-time exception:[0m use React only for the recipe pages and keep D1 for the rest of the site.
  3. [1mKeep D1:[0m stay with plain HTML and CSS.

  If you choose React, do you want it built into static pages ahead of time, or running in the browser? I'll
  record your answer in the ledger and then update T1.

